{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm.macro — Macro Expansion as a Stream Process\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: agreed design target (2026-09-13). This document is the macro\n     4\tcontract for `yin.vm`. It supersedes the v1 stream model in\n     5\t[`macro-design.md`](./macro-design.md), the strategy in\n     6\t[`../cross-language-macro.md`](../cross-language-macro.md), and the previous\n     7\trevision of this document, which integrated expansion into the evaluators;\n     8\tAppendix A records what that revision proposed and why it is withdrawn. It\n     9\tis subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md) and\n    10\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md). Revision 3 folds in the round-2 reviews\n    11\t(`collab/1789277584941-review-macro-stream-process-r2.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`);\n    12\tAppendix C maps each finding to its resolution.\n    13\t\n    14\tThe design follows from one framing and adds nothing to it:\n    15\t\n    16\t> Every `yin.vm` evaluator is an observer of a `dao.stream` medium. The\n    17\t> writer to that medium is just another process. Macro expansion is such a\n    18\t> process.\n    19\t\n    20\t---\n    21\t\n    22\t## 0. Decisions\n    23\t\n    24\t1. **Evaluators know nothing about macros.** No evaluator — `ast-walker`,\n    25\t   `linearize`/`semantic`, or a future stack VM — has a macro transition, a\n    26\t   macro instruction, a macro flag on closures, a macro option at\n    27\t   construction, or an expansion ledger. An evaluator executes semantic\n    28\t   datoms and rejects, at load, any node type outside its vocabulary.\n    29\t2. **Expansion is a process between two media.** The expander observes a\n    30\t   *source* medium (`program-in`), rewrites each batch to a fixpoint, and\n    31\t   appends the result to a *program* medium (`program-out`) that evaluators\n    32\t   observe. It is a forwarder in the sense of `dao.stream.md` §Composition,\n    33\t   driven by the same `stream-observer/run-on-stream` coordination that\n    34\t   drives an evaluator.\n    35\t3. **There is no phase.** \"Compile time\" and \"runtime\" named which cursor\n    36\t   reached a batch first. With expansion on its own medium, the distinction\n    37\t   has no referent; `:yin/phase-policy` and `:yin/phase` are retired.\n    38\t4. **A macro call site is an ordinary application.** In datom form, every\n    39\t   application's operands are already unevaluated syntax, so nothing marks a\n    40\t   call site. The expander rewrites an `:application` whose operator\n    41\t   resolves, under lexical shadowing, to a lambda carrying `:yin/macro? true`.\n    42\t   `:yin/macro-expand` is retired from the Universal AST.\n    43\t5. **Definitions never reach an evaluator.** The expander consumes every\n    44\t   macro definition in a batch into its own store and forwards only expanded\n    45\t   program. A macro name reaching an evaluator is therefore an unbound\n    46\t   variable — a loud failure — never a function applied to evaluated\n    47\t   arguments. Every `yin/def` is authoritative for the expander's store: a\n    48\t   plain redefinition of a macro name removes the macro (§3.1).\n    49\t6. **Macros are closed syntax transformers.** A macro body receives operand\n    50\t   ASTs bound to its parameters and returns a plain-data AST. It sees no\n    51\t   program store, no captured environment, no streams. Expansion is a pure\n    52\t   function of (macro lambda, operand ASTs, allocation state) on every host.\n    53\t7. **Expansion is outermost-first and re-expands its own output to a\n    54\t   fixpoint** under dual guards (depth, output size per expansion; steps per\n    55\t   body; optional cumulative bound per batch) with cycle detection.\n    56\t8. **Output batches are self-contained.** Unchanged operands are copied,\n    57\t   not referenced, into `program-out`; refs across media are meaningful only\n    58\t   for durable ids, and the expander does not pretend otherwise.\n    59\t9. **Provenance is a third medium.** Every attempt produces a\n    60\t   `:macro-expand-event`; events and the `m`-tagged output copies are\n    61\t   appended to an optional *log* medium. Evaluators never see events.\n    62\t   `program-out` datoms carry `default-op` only.\n    63\t11. **An expansion failure is data, not a throw.** A batch that fails to\n    64\t    expand yields an error result: its event goes to the log, nothing goes to\n    65\t    `program-out`, the source cursor advances, and the driver receives the\n    66\t    error. Throws are reserved for defects of the forwarder itself.\n    67\t10. **Authority is write authority on a medium.** Whoever may append to\n    68\t    `program-in` may define macros; whoever holds the `program-out` writer\n    69\t    decides what programs run. No VM option encodes this.\n    70\t\n    71\t---\n    72\t\n    73\t## 1. Axioms and invariants\n    74\t\n    75\t**Interpretation creates semantics.** A `:lambda` entity with\n    76\t`:yin/macro? true` is an ordinary lambda. An `:application` whose operator\n    77\tnames it is an ordinary application. What makes the pair a macro and a call\n    78\tsite is *which process reads them*: the expander reads them as a rewrite\n    79\trule and a rewrite site; an evaluator, if it ever saw them, would read them\n    80\tas a function and a call. The design keeps them on the expander's side of a\n    81\tmedium boundary, so the evaluator never has to know the difference.\n    82\t\n    83\t**Code and state are datoms.** Definitions, call sites, expansion output,\n    84\tand events are all `:yin/*` and `:macro-expand-event` datoms. Original call\n    85\tdatoms on `program-in` are never rewritten; `program-out` is a new, complete\n    86\tprogram. The link from a call to its expansion is forward-only, through the\n    87\tevent.\n    88\t\n    89\t**Everything is a stream.** The expander is a forwarder: one batch in, one\n    90\tbatch out, `full` retained for retry, cadence owned by the driver. Nothing\n    91\tabout it requires support from the stream contract or from any evaluator.\n    92\tAn evaluator observing `program-out` is exactly the evaluator observing any\n    93\tprogram medium.\n    94\t\n    95\t**Everything is a continuation.** Nothing here touches continuations. A\n    96\tcontinuation captured by an evaluator references only semantic datoms,\n    97\twhich is why a shipped continuation needs no expander at its destination.\n    98\t\n    99\t| Invariant | How the design honours it |\n   100\t|---|---|\n   101\t| No hidden global state | The macro environment is the expander's own store, a value in its process state. Fresh ids and gensyms come from an allocation counter seeded from each batch and returned as a watermark. No registry, no `defonce`. |\n   102\t| No implicit control flow | Expansion happens in exactly one place: the expander's step. Nothing expands on lookup, load, apply, or transition. |\n   103\t| No callbacks | The expander is a function over a batch and a context returning data; its driver is `run-on-stream`. The body runner is a function called synchronously on a throwaway VM value. |\n   104\t| No shared mutable state | Expander state is a persistent map threaded through the step. The one local cell — a gensym counter inside one body invocation — cannot escape because the output validator rejects any host value. |\n   105\t| No layer collapsing | *compile* (yang) → medium → *expand* → medium → *load/execute*. Each is a function over data. The evaluator never re-enters expansion; the expander never holds the program evaluator's control state (its own throwaway body runner is private to it). |\n   106\t| No assumed graphs | The expander indexes each batch with `index-datoms` explicitly, checks the reachable graph acyclic and every ref resolvable at admission, visits ref attributes in a fixed order, and honours the explicit `:yin/root` fact. Malformed input, dangling roots, and forged ids are errors naming the entity. |\n   107\t\n   108\t---\n   109\t\n   110\t## 2. Representation\n   111\t\n   112\t### 2.1 The macro entity\n   113\t\n   114\t| Attribute | Value | Note |\n   115\t|---|---|---|\n   116\t| `:yin/type` | `:lambda` | structurally a lambda |\n   117\t| `:yin/params` | vector of symbols; `&` before the last permitted | variadic binding is the expander's (§3.3) |\n   118\t| `:yin/body` | ref | |\n   119\t| `:yin/macro?` | `true` | the only fact that distinguishes a macro |\n   120\t\n   121\tAST form `{:type :lambda :params [...] :body ... :macro? true}`. `yang.clojure`\n   122\tlowers `(defmacro name [params] body)` — at any nesting — to\n   123\t`(yin/def name <lambda macro? true>)`, exactly as `def` lowers today with the\n   124\tflag added. `^{:yang/shadow-params-operand}` / `^{:yang/shadow-body-start}`\n   125\tmetadata is retired (§3.2 explains why outermost-first expansion makes the\n   126\thint unnecessary).\n   127\t\n   128\t### 2.2 The definition form\n   129\t\n   130\tA **definition** is an `:application` whose operator is\n   131\t`{:type :variable :name yin/def}` and whose operands are\n   132\t`[{:type :literal :value <sym>} <lambda macro? true>]` — the shape\n   133\t`yang.clojure/compile-def` already emits. The expander recognises this shape\n   134\tanywhere in a batch (§3.1).\n   135\t\n   136\t**Two namespaces.** The expander's store and an evaluator's store are\n   137\tdisjoint and never reconciled. Defining a macro `m` does not remove an\n   138\tearlier function `m` from an evaluator's store — it cannot; they are on\n   139\tdifferent media — but every later `(m …)` is rewritten before it reaches the\n   140\tevaluator, so the old binding is reachable only through `m` in operand\n   141\tposition. Conversely, a plain `(yin/def m <lambda>)` after a `defmacro m`\n   142\tremoves `m` from the expander's store *and* is forwarded, so the evaluator\n   143\tbinds the function. Neither direction is silent: the expander's store is\n   144\tauthoritative for what expands, the evaluator's for what runs.\n   145\t\n   146\t### 2.3 The call site\n   147\t\n   148\tAny `:application` whose operator is a `:variable` naming a macro in scope,\n   149\tor is itself a `:lambda` with `:macro? true`. Operands are whatever the\n   150\tfrontend compiled them to — canonical Universal AST nodes, never\n   151\tsource-language forms. A Python `defn(\"f\", [\"x\"], x)` and a Clojure\n   152\t`(defn f [x] x)` are both `:application`s; the frontend does no discovery and\n   153\tno coercion. **Contract for macro authors:** use the prelude accessors\n   154\t(`yin/name-of`) for names rather than matching on `:type`, because a name\n   155\tmay arrive as a `:variable`, a `:literal` symbol, or a `:literal` string.\n   156\t\n   157\t### 2.4 The root fact and the codec\n   158\t\n   159\t`ast->datoms-with-root` appends `[root :yin/root true t default-op]`;\n   160\t`index-datoms` takes the *last* `:yin/root` fact in batch order as the\n   161\troot, falls back to the existing heuristic when none is present, and fails\n   162\t`{:rule :dangling-root :entity e}` if the named entity has no `:yin/type`. An\n   163\texplicit `:root-id` option still overrides. This is a codec improvement\n   164\tindependent of macros; the expander relies on it because it emits copies\n   165\tbottom-up and must not depend on emission order for root discovery.\n   166\t\n   167\tThe codec (`yin.vm/ast->datoms`, `datoms->ast`) keeps `:yin/macro?` on\n   168\tlambdas and gains `:yin/root`. It drops `:yin/macro-expand`,\n   169\t`:yin/phase-policy`, `:yin/phase`, `:yin/capability` and the\n   170\t`:macro-expand-event` attributes: events are written by the expander through\n   171\tits own small emitter (§4.1), not through the AST codec, because they are\n   172\tnot AST.\n   173\t\n   174\t---\n   175\t\n   176\t## 3. The expander (`yin.vm.macro`)\n   177\t\n   178\t### 3.1 Contract\n   179\t\n   180\t```\n   181\texpand-batch : datoms ctx → {:status :ok     :datoms out :root-eid r :log [...] :ctx ctx'}\n   182\t                          | {:status :error  :error {:kind ..} :log [...] :ctx ctx'}\n   183\texpand       : datoms ctx → out | throws                ; convenience over expand-batch\n   184\tdefinitions  : datoms → {sym lambda-ast|nil}           ; syntactic harvest, last wins; nil = removal\n   185\tinvoke       : lambda-ast operand-asts ctx → value      ; one body, §3.3\n   186\tvalid-ast?   : value opts → nil | {:kind :invalid-output :at path}\n   187\tmark-tail    : ast → ast                               ; §3.4, whole-tree recompute\n   188\tstep         : expander → expander'                    ; one forwarder step, §5\n   189\tevent-schema                                           ; §4.1\n   190\tstdlib-forms, prelude\n   191\t```\n   192\t\n   193\t`ctx` is the expander's process state and is threaded through every call:\n   194\t\n   195\t```\n   196\t{:store    {sym lambda-ast}       ; macro environment, persisted across batches\n   197\t :alloc    {:next-eid n}          ; monotonic negative counter, never re-seeded upward\n   198\t :guards   {:max-depth 100  :max-nodes-per-expansion 10000\n   199\t            :max-steps 100000  :max-datoms-per-batch nil}\n   200\t :eval     (fn [lambda-ast env prelude budget] → value)   ; §3.3\n   201\t :t        0}                     ; batch counter; incremented once per expand-batch\n   202\t```\n   203\t\n   204\t`expand-batch`:\n   205\t\n   206\t1. **Admit.** The batch is indexed with `index-datoms`; every ref is checked\n   207\t   to resolve to an entity in the index and the reachable graph from the root\n   208\t   is checked acyclic, *before* any recursive decoding (Appendix B). Every\n   209\t   ground value in the batch is checked plain-data (§3.3) — an in-memory\n   210\t   medium can carry host values, and the sandbox's closed-input guarantee\n   211\t   holds only if nothing else is admitted. A failure here is a forwarder\n   212\t   result `{:status :error :error {:kind :malformed-input ...}}`.\n   213\t2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `yin/def` form whose value is a\n   214\t   `:lambda`, in batch order, last wins: a `:macro? true` lambda is\n   215\t   `assoc`ed into `:store`; a plain lambda `dissoc`es the name, so a\n   216\t   redefinition as a function stops expansion of that name. Harvest is\n   217\t   *syntactic* and whole-batch by policy: a definition in an unexecuted\n   218\t   branch, or after its first use in the same batch, is still in force for\n   219\t   the whole batch. (Clojure defines a macro only when the `defmacro` form\n   220\t   executes; this design does not, and says so.)\n   221\t3. **Replace.** Each macro definition node is replaced by\n   222\t   `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`, so a batch whose root is a definition\n   223\t   still forwards a complete program and a REPL prints the name. Plain\n   224\t   `yin/def`s are forwarded untouched.\n   225\t4. **Expand.** `expand-node` (§3.2) runs from the root over the index.\n   226\t5. **Mark tail.** `mark-tail` recomputes `:tail?` over the whole final tree\n   227\t   (§3.4).\n   228\t6. **Emit.** Output is emitted bottom-up as a self-contained batch with a\n   229\t   fresh `:yin/root` fact; the log copy carries `m` per §4.1.\n   230\t7. **Scan.** A final walk asserts no lambda with `:yin/macro? true` remains.\n   231\t   Fixpoint for applications is by construction (§3.2); this scan is the\n   232\t   guard for decision 5.\n   233\t\n   234\tAn `:error` result carries the attempt event (§4.1) in `:log` and the\n   235\tadvanced `:ctx`; `:datoms` is absent. `expand` unwraps `:ok` and throws on\n   236\t`:error` for callers that want the exception. Same inputs, same output, on\n   237\tevery host.\n   238\t\n   239\t**Working representation.** Traversal, cycle detection, and shadow tracking\n   240\trun over *eids in the index*. Plain AST maps are materialised only at the\n   241\tboundary of one body invocation — operands decoded for `invoke`, the returned\n   242\tvalue validated and re-encoded to datoms with fresh ids, and re-expansion\n   243\tcontinues over the index of the batch-so-far. Internal identity never\n   244\tappears in the map representation macros see (`valid-ast?` rejects `:eid`).\n   245\t\n   246\t**Allocation.** `:alloc` is seeded per batch to\n   247\t`(min (:next-eid alloc) (dec min-eid-in-batch) (- (inc datom/first-user-id)))`\n   248\t— the incoming watermark is a term, so the counter only ever decreases across\n   249\ta session and event eids on the accumulated log never collide. It is returned\n   250\tadvanced in both `:ok` and `:error` results; a retry of a staged flush reuses\n   251\tthe staged allocation and never allocates again.\n   252\t\n   253\t### 3.2 The algorithm\n   254\t\n   255\tTraversal visits ref attributes in a **fixed order** so fresh ids and\n   256\tgensyms are deterministic across hosts: `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`\n   257\t(left to right), `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`,\n   258\t`:yin/body`, `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`. `shadow` is the\n   259\tset of names bound by enclosing `:lambda :params`.\n   260\t\n   261\t```\n   262\tmacro-of ctx eid shadow :\n   263\t  case operator(eid)\n   264\t    :variable v  → v ∉ shadow ∧ (:store ctx) v\n   265\t    :lambda  l   → l when (:macro? l)\n   266\t    _            → nil\n   267\t\n   268\texpand-node ctx eid shadow depth :\n   269\t  if type(eid) = :application ∧ (m = macro-of ctx eid shadow):\n   270\t      ev        = fresh-event ctx eid m                  ; allocated first: every failure below is recorded on ev\n   271\t      if depth ≥ :max-depth: fail ev {:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}\n   272\t      operands  = [decode c | c ∈ operands(eid)]         ; unexpanded AST maps\n   273\t      out       = invoke m operands ctx                  ; §3.3 — arity, fuel, suspension fail on ev\n   274\t      check valid-ast? out {:allow-macro-lambda? false}  ; node count ≤ :max-nodes-per-expansion\n   275\t      root'     = encode out (fresh ids from :alloc)     ; joins the index of the batch-so-far\n   276\t      record ev :yin/expansion-root root'\n   277\t      return expand-node ctx root' shadow (depth+1)      ; re-expand the output under the call site's scope\n   278\t  else:\n   279\t      shadow'   = if type(eid) = :lambda then shadow ∪ params(eid) else shadow\n   280\t      children' = [expand-node ctx c shadow' depth | c ∈ children(eid) in fixed order]\n   281\t      eid'      = if children' = children(eid) then eid else copy eid with children'\n   282\t      if type(eid') = :application ∧ macro-of ctx eid' shadow:\n   283\t          return expand-node ctx eid' shadow depth        ; the operator became a macro name — re-check once\n   284\t      return eid'\n   285\t```\n   286\t\n   287\t`fail ev …` writes `:yin/error` on the event and unwinds to `expand-batch`,\n   288\twhich returns the `:error` result with that event in `:log`. Cycle and\n   289\tdangling-ref detection happened at admission (§3.1 step 1), so this walk is\n   290\tover a known-acyclic graph; output encoded from validated plain maps is\n   291\tacyclic by construction.\n   292\t\n   293\tProperties, each a Phase 1 test:\n   294\t\n   295\t- **Outermost first.** A macro sees its operands as written. This is what\n   296\t  makes `->`-style macros expressible and what retires the shadow hints:\n   297\t  in `(defn f [x] (x 1))`, `defn` expands *before* its operands are examined,\n   298\t  yielding `(yin/def f (lambda [x] (x 1)))`; re-expansion then meets `(x 1)`\n   299\t  under `:lambda [x]`, so `x` is shadowed and left alone even if a macro\n   300\t  named `x` exists. yang's frontend hint existed only because the frontend\n   301\t  had to decide this before expansion.\n   302\t- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root\n   303\t  was visited after its last rewrite, *including* an application whose\n   304\t  operator was rewritten into a macro name or an inline macro lambda by a\n   305\t  child expansion (`((choose) x)` where `choose` expands to `m`): the\n   306\t  ordinary branch re-checks the rebuilt node once, and that re-check\n   307\t  expands at the same depth, so it cannot loop without consuming depth.\n   308\t- **Depth** counts nesting of expansion inside expansion output. Direct\n   309\t  recursion without a base case fails at 100 with\n   310\t  `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`. Siblings do not\n   311\t  accumulate depth.\n   312\t- **Shadowing is lexical and complete.** `let`, `do`, and every binder every\n   313\t  frontend emits lower to `:lambda :params`; `yin/def` is global and is the\n   314\t  store. There is no other binder in the Universal AST. The shadow set is\n   315\t  extended *at the lambda node*, so an operator-position lambda's params\n   316\t  shadow its body and not its sibling operands.\n   317\t- **Generated definitions are rejected in v1.** A macro whose output\n   318\t  contains a `:macro? true` lambda fails `{:kind :invalid-output :reason\n   319\t  :generated-definition}`; `valid-ast?` is called with\n   320\t  `:allow-macro-lambda? false` on expansion output. Macro-defining macros\n   321\t  are reserved (§7) until the precedence of definitions introduced\n   322\t  mid-batch is specified.\n   323\t- **Failure leaves a record.** The attempt event is allocated before the\n   324\t  depth guard, resolution, and invocation, so every failure after that\n   325\t  point is written with `:yin/error` (§4.1) and reaches the log through the\n   326\t  `:error` result (§5).\n   327\t\n   328\t### 3.3 Executing the macro lambda\n   329\t\n   330\t`invoke` binds positional params one AST map each and `& rest` to a vector\n   331\tof the remaining maps (arity mismatch is\n   332\t`{:kind :arity :macro eid :expected n :got m}`) and calls\n   333\t`((:eval ctx) lambda-ast env prelude budget)`.\n   334\t\n   335\tThe expander supplies its own body runner; today it is a throwaway\n   336\t`yin.vm.ast-walker` driven by `step` in a counted loop, because\n   337\t`engine/run-loop` has no fuel and a body that loops forever would otherwise\n   338\tstall the forwarder:\n   339\t\n   340\t```\n   341\t(defn run-bounded [lambda-ast env prelude budget]\n   342\t  (let [vm (ast-walker/create-vm {:env env :primitives (merge vm/primitives prelude)})\n   343\t        vm (ast-walker/vm-load-program vm (vm/ast->datoms (:body lambda-ast)))]\n   344\t    (loop [vm vm, n 0]\n   345\t      (cond (> n budget)            (throw (ex-info \"Macro body exceeded step budget\" {:kind :fuel-guard :steps n}))\n   346\t            (vm/blocked? vm)        (throw (ex-info \"Macro body suspended\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   347\t            (seq (:parked vm))      (throw (ex-info \"Macro body parked\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   348\t            (seq (:ready-queue vm)) (throw (ex-info \"Macro body scheduled work\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   349\t            (vm/halted? vm)         (vm/value vm)\n   350\t            :else                   (recur (vm/step vm) (inc n))))))\n   351\t```\n   352\t\n   353\tThis is a private detail of the expander, not a VM feature: the throwaway\n   354\tVM is an ordinary evaluator running an ordinary lambda. It has no\n   355\t`:make-stream` and no FFI pair, so stream and FFI effects fail with the\n   356\texisting constructor errors; `yin/def` inside a body writes to the throwaway\n   357\tstore and is discarded; `require` resolves against `nil` modules and fails.\n   358\tThe body sees only its parameters, the prelude, and `yin.vm/primitives` —\n   359\tdecision 6. Macro authors are told plainly: *a macro body may not call a\n   360\tfunction the program defined, and may not call another macro: the body is\n   361\texecuted, not expanded, so a macro name inside it is an unbound variable.\n   362\tHelpers for macros are prelude calls.* Expanding macro bodies before\n   363\tstoring them (which would allow macro helpers) is reserved (§7).\n   364\t\n   365\t`:eval` and the prelude are trusted composition capabilities under a stated\n   366\tcontract: deterministic, no host IO, no retention of arguments. The fuel\n   367\tbudget bounds VM transitions, not the cost of one host primitive or the\n   368\ttraversal of one large literal; `:max-nodes-per-expansion` bounds AST nodes,\n   369\tnot literal payload size. Both are known limits of v1, recorded here rather\n   370\tthan solved: a composition that admits untrusted macro *authors* needs a\n   371\tpayload bound at admission (§3.1 step 1), which is where such a bound\n   372\tbelongs.\n   373\t\n   374\t\"Run a lambda under a step budget in a fresh VM\" is the one capability the\n   375\texpander needs that `yin.vm` does not export today. It is a general\n   376\tfacility — any process that runs another's code wants it — and is the\n   377\treason the expander can later become a `yin.vm` program itself (§7,\n   378\tReserved) without any macro-specific primitive.\n   379\t\n   380\t**Validation.** The same `valid-ast?` runs on what goes *in* (every operand\n   381\tAST decoded for a body, and every ground value at admission) and on what\n   382\tcomes *out* (the body's value): a closed, plain-data Universal AST. It\n   383\tenforces, per node, exactly the vocabulary `ast->datoms-with-root` emits:\n   384\t\n   385\t| `:type` | Required | Optional |\n   386\t|---|---|---|\n   387\t| `:literal` | `:value` plain data | `:tail?` |\n   388\t| `:variable` | `:name` symbol | `:tail?` |\n   389\t| `:lambda` | `:params` vector of symbols, `:body` | `:tail?` `:macro?` |\n   390\t| `:application` | `:operator`, `:operands` vector | `:tail?` |\n   391\t| `:if` | `:test` `:consequent` `:alternate` | `:tail?` |\n   392\t| `:dao.stream.apply/call` | `:op` keyword, `:operands` | `:tail?` |\n   393\t| `:vm/gensym` | | `:prefix` string |\n   394\t| `:vm/store-get` | `:key` | |\n   395\t| `:vm/store-put` | `:key` `:val` plain data | |\n   396\t| `:vm/park`, `:vm/current-continuation` | | |\n   397\t| `:vm/resume` | `:parked-id` keyword, `:val` | |\n   398\t| `:stream/make` | | `:buffer` int |\n   399\t| `:stream/put` | `:target` `:val` | |\n   400\t| `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`, `:stream/close` | `:source` | |\n   401\t\n   402\tAny other `:type`, any key outside the row, any `:eid`, a `:macro? true`\n   403\tlambda where `:allow-macro-lambda?` is false, or any non-plain value\n   404\tanywhere (recursively through vectors, maps, and sets, including inside\n   405\t`:literal :value`) is `{:kind :invalid-output :macro eid :at [path]}` (or\n   406\t`:malformed-input` at admission).\n   407\t*Plain data* is nil, booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, symbols, and\n   408\tvectors/maps/sets thereof. `:vm/store-update` is absent: it is a walker-only\n   409\ttransition with no datom form. The walk also counts nodes for the\n   410\tper-expansion bound, checked *before* emission so oversized output never\n   411\tmaterialises.\n   412\t\n   413\t**The prelude** — primitives merged into the throwaway VM for one body:\n   414\t\n   415\t| Primitive | Meaning |\n   416\t|---|---|\n   417\t| `yin/node-type ast`, `yin/node-get ast k` | accessors |\n   418\t| `yin/literal v`, `yin/variable sym` | leaf constructors |\n   419\t| `yin/lambda params body`, `yin/application op operands`, `yin/if t c a` | node constructors |\n   420\t| `yin/sequence-body [ast ...]` | the `do` desugaring yang uses; empty → `nil` literal |\n   421\t| `yin/make-lambda params-ast body-ast` | `params-ast` is a `:literal` whose value is the param vector |\n   422\t| `yin/make-def name-ast value-ast` | `(yin/def <sym> value)`; `name-ast` may be `:variable`, `:literal` symbol, or `:literal` string |\n   423\t| `yin/name-of ast` | the symbol named by any of those three shapes; error otherwise |\n   424\t| `yin/gensym-sym prefix` | fresh symbol `prefix__<E>_<n>`, `E` = absolute event eid (unique across a session by the allocation rule, §3.1), `n` invocation-local |\n   425\t\n   426\tNo constructor marks tail positions (§3.4). A macro that wants to emit a\n   427\tcall to another macro emits an ordinary `yin/application`; re-expansion\n   428\thandles it. **Hygiene** is non-hygienic by default with `yin/gensym-sym` on\n   429\trequest; automatic hygiene is reserved.\n   430\t\n   431\t`stdlib-forms` (the `defn` macro) is recovered from\n   432\t`d8b27a5^:src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc` with its shadow metadata removed:\n   433\t\n   434\t```clojure\n   435\t'[(defmacro defn [fn-name fn-params & body]\n   436\t    (let [b (yin/sequence-body body)\n   437\t          l (yin/make-lambda fn-params b)]\n   438\t      (yin/make-def fn-name l)))]\n   439\t```\n   440\t\n   441\t### 3.4 Tail positions in expansion output\n   442\t\n   443\tTail-ness is contextual, so constructors cannot mark it, and a macro can\n   444\tmove syntax that *was* in tail position into an operand or a test. `mark-tail\n   445\tast` is therefore one deterministic pass over the **whole final tree** after\n   446\texpansion (§3.1 step 5) that *recomputes* every `:tail?` — setting it where\n   447\tthe computed context is tail and **removing** it where it is not. The\n   448\tcontext rules, one per accepted node type:\n   449\t\n   450\t- the program root is tail;\n   451\t- `:if`: `:test` is non-tail; `:consequent` and `:alternate` inherit;\n   452\t- `:application`: `:operator` and every operand are non-tail; the node\n   453\t  itself carries its context's flag;\n   454\t- `:lambda`: the body is tail — always, relative to the lambda's own return,\n   455\t  regardless of where the lambda sits. An immediately applied lambda\n   456\t  (yang's `let`/`do`) is a lambda; `linearize` decides whether to inline it,\n   457\t  and if it does, it owns the consequence;\n   458\t- `:dao.stream.apply/call`, `:stream/put`, `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`,\n   459\t  `:stream/close`, `:vm/resume`: every child is non-tail; the node carries\n   460\t  its context's flag;\n   461\t- leaves and childless nodes carry their context's flag.\n   462\t\n   463\tThe walker never reads `:tail?`; `linearize` and the stack VM do. Without\n   464\tthis pass a tail-position macro expanding to an ordinary application would\n   465\tpush one frame per iteration on the semantic VM while the walker stayed\n   466\tflat; with an *additive* pass, a stale flag on syntax moved into an operand\n   467\twould let the semantic VM drop a continuation it still needs.\n   468\t\n   469\t### 3.5 Guards\n   470\t\n   471\t| Guard | Scope | Default | Error |\n   472\t|---|---|---|---|\n   473\t| depth | per expansion chain | 100 | `{:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}` |\n   474\t| nodes per expansion | one macro's output | 10,000 | `{:kind :datom-guard :scope :expansion :emitted :call}` |\n   475\t| datoms per batch | cumulative over one `expand-batch` | unlimited | `{:kind :datom-guard :scope :batch :emitted}` |\n   476\t| steps | one body invocation | 100,000 | `{:kind :fuel-guard :steps}` |\n   477\t| cyclic / dangling / host-valued input | admission | — | `{:kind :malformed-input :reason :cyclic|:dangling-ref|:host-value :eid}` |\n   478\t| arity / output / suspended | — | — | `{:kind :arity}` `{:kind :invalid-output :at}` `{:kind :suspended}` |\n   479\t\n   480\tGuard failures are `:error` results (decision 11); `:malformed-input` is\n   481\ttoo, so a corrupt batch is logged and skipped rather than re-read forever.\n   482\t\n   483\t---\n   484\t\n   485\t## 4. Provenance\n   486\t\n   487\t### 4.1 The expansion event\n   488\t\n   489\tEvery attempt, successful or not, produces one event. Events and the\n   490\t`m`-tagged output are appended to the **log** medium, when the expander has\n   491\tone; `program-out` receives only program.\n   492\t\n   493\t```\n   494\t[ev :yin/type           :macro-expand-event  t default-op]\n   495\t[ev :yin/source-batch   <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; which source batch; qualifies source-call\n   496\t[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]   ; eid in that batch, or in the log copy of the enclosing expansion\n   497\t[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; when the operator was a :variable; absent for an inline macro lambda\n   498\t[ev :yin/macro          <lambda-eid>         t default-op]   ; only when durable (§4.2)\n   499\t[ev :yin/expansion-root <root'>              t default-op]   ; on success; names the LOG copy\n   500\t[ev :yin/error          {:kind ...}          t default-op]   ; on failure\n   501\t[ev :yin/timestamp      <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; logical, never a host clock\n   502\t```\n   503\t\n   504\tThe log copy of expansion output carries `m = ev`; log copies of unchanged\n   505\toperands and ancestors carry `m = default-op` (re-parenting, not macro\n   506\toutput). **`program-out` datoms all carry `default-op`**: `m = ev` would be\n   507\ta cross-medium ref (§4.2). A nested expansion's `:yin/source-call` names a\n   508\tnode in the log copy of the enclosing expansion's output, which is how the\n   509\tchain source → event → root → event → root is walked for generated syntax.\n   510\t\"Every datom this expansion produced\" is `[?d _ _ _ ?ev]` on the log.\n   511\t\n   512\t`yin.vm.macro/event-schema` declares these attributes (`:yin/source-call`,\n   513\t`:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root` as refs) for compositions that commit a\n   514\tlog to `dao.space`; the transactor relocates only declared refs, so a\n   515\tcomposition merges this fragment with `yin.vm/schema` before committing.\n   516\t\n   517\t### 4.2 Identity across media\n   518\t\n   519\t`program-in`, `program-out`, and the log are different media. A ref written\n   520\ton one that names an entity on another is meaningful only when the named\n   521\tid is **durable** — a permanent positive id from a committed stream. The\n   522\ttransactor (`dao.space.transact`) resolves tempids within one transaction;\n   523\tcommitting two batches separately does not connect them. Therefore:\n   524\t\n   525\t- `program-out` batches are self-contained: unchanged subtrees are copied,\n   526\t  with fresh ids, not referenced (decision 8).\n   527\t- `:yin/source-call` on an event names the call's eid *as it appeared in\n   528\t  the source batch*, qualified by `:yin/source-batch`; it is descriptive\n   529\t  until both media are committed under one resolution. `:yin/macro` is\n   530\t  written only when the macro's id is durable; otherwise `:yin/macro-name`\n   531\t  carries the name. Names are metadata, never identity repair.\n   532\t- `:yin/expansion-root` names the **log copy** of the output. The executed\n   533\t  program on `program-out` is a distinct copy with distinct ids; this design\n   534\t  promises no id-level correspondence between them. A composition that needs\n   535\t  one commits program and log in one transaction (they are one batch to the\n   536\t  transactor) or records the mapping itself.\n   537\t- A pipeline that wants queryable provenance commits `program-in` first and\n   538\t  expands against durable ids — the ordering rule of\n   539\t  `cross-language-macro.md` §4, retained. `ADR 0003` makes `dao.space` the\n   540\t  medium for that commit.\n   541\t\n   542\t### 4.3 Authority\n   543\t\n   544\tWrite authority on a medium is the whole model. Defining a macro is\n   545\tappending a definition to `program-in`; running a program is appending to\n   546\t`program-out`; both are decided by whoever holds the writer, and\n   547\t`dao.stream.md` puts that decision in the composition. A Shibi\n   548\tcapability, when it exists, is verified at the medium's boundary before the\n   549\tappend, not inside any VM. The old `:macro-authorize` VM option is gone\n   550\tbecause there is nothing left inside a VM to authorize.\n   551\t\n   552\t---\n   553\t\n   554\t## 5. The expander as an observer\n   555\t\n   556\tThe expander is driven by `yin.vm.stream-observer/run-on-stream` with\n   557\tits state in the `:vm` slot — the coordination inspects no evaluator field,\n   558\tso it drives an expander as readily as a VM:\n   559\t\n   560\t```\n   561\texpander = {:ctx ctx\n   562\t            :out writer        :out-staged  nil|datoms\n   563\t            :log writer|nil    :log-staged  nil|datoms\n   564\t            :errors []}        ; expansion failures this round, for the driver\n   565\t\n   566\tready?       (fn [x] (and (nil? (:out-staged x)) (nil? (:log-staged x))))\n   567\tload-program (fn [x batch]\n   568\t               (let [r (expand-batch batch (:ctx x))]\n   569\t                 (-> x (assoc :ctx (:ctx r))\n   570\t                       (assoc :out-staged (when (= :ok (:status r)) (:datoms r)))\n   571\t                       (assoc :log-staged (when (:log x) (:log r)))\n   572\t                       (cond-> (= :error (:status r)) (update :errors conj (:error r))))))\n   573\trun-vm       flush                    ; below\n   574\t```\n   575\t\n   576\t**Flush** delivers each staged payload to its own medium and clears *that*\n   577\tslot on `ok`, independently:\n   578\t\n   579\t| Outcome on a medium | Effect on its slot |\n   580\t|---|---|\n   581\t| `ok` | slot ← nil |\n   582\t| `full` | slot kept; expander stays not-ready; the same payload is retried next round |\n   583\t| `closed`, `invalid-value`, `transport-error` | throw — a forwarder or composition defect; the other slot's state is preserved in the thrown value |\n   584\t\n   585\tOnce a medium has answered `ok` its payload is never appended again by an\n   586\tordinary retry, so `out = ok, log = full` re-tries only the log. A batch\n   587\tthat expands with `:error` stages nothing for `program-out` and stages the\n   588\tevent for the log; the source cursor advances (the load returned), the\n   589\tfailure is on the log, and the driver reads `:errors`. Program and log are\n   590\tindependent media: this design offers *at-most-once per medium* and batch\n   591\torder per medium, not atomic visibility across the two; a composition that\n   592\tneeds atomicity commits both to one `dao.space` transaction (§4.2).\n   593\t\n   594\t`run-on-stream`'s contract supplies the rest: a `full` leaves the expander\n   595\tnot-ready with the exact payload staged, so the next round retries the\n   596\tappend before observing again; `gap` on `program-in` is counted by the\n   597\tobserver and is the composition's policy, as for any observer. What remains\n   598\ta *throw* out of `load-program` is a forwarder defect (codec failure, index\n   599\tfailure); the cursor does not advance and the batch is re-read, which is\n   600\tthe correct behaviour for a bug and the wrong one for bad input — hence\n   601\tdecision 11.\n   602\t\n   603\t**Prerequisite on the observer (Phase 0).** `run-on-stream` today publishes\n   604\tits successor session only on return. If it forwards A, then B's load or run\n   605\tthrows, the caller still holds the pre-A session and a retry forwards A again\n   606\t(reproduced by the round-2 review against the current observer). This is a\n   607\tdefect of the generic coordination, not of this design — a VM loader that\n   608\tthrows on B after loading A has the same problem — and it is fixed there:\n   609\t`run-on-stream` must surface an error *with* the session state reached so\n   610\tfar (`{:observer :vm :error}`), so the caller resumes from A's successor.\n   611\tThis document depends on that fix and does not work around it.\n   612\t\n   613\t```\n   614\t  yang ──▶ program-in ──observe──▶ expander ──append──▶ program-out ──observe──▶ evaluator\n   615\t                                       │\n   616\t                                       └──append──▶ log (events, m-tagged output)\n   617\t```\n   618\t\n   619\tEvery evaluator observes `program-out` through its unchanged loader:\n   620\t`ast-walker/vm-load-program`, `linearize/loader ∘ semantic`, the stack\n   621\tcompiler. None composes anything macro-related.\n   622\t\n   623\t---\n   624\t\n   625\t## 6. Compositions\n   626\t\n   627\t### 6.1 `yin.repl`\n   628\t\n   629\t`make-session` builds `program-in`, the expander (with `stdlib-forms`\n   630\tpre-loaded into its store), `program-out`, an optional log medium, and the\n   631\tevaluator's observer on `program-out`. An evaluation round is: append the\n   632\tfrontend's datoms to `program-in`; drive the expander with `run-on-stream`\n   633\tuntil ready; if `:errors` is non-empty, print them as the round's result\n   634\t(the batch was consumed; nothing ran); otherwise drive the evaluator with\n   635\t`run-on-stream` on `program-out`; drain output. `eval-ast` takes this same\n   636\tpath (`vm/eval` is no longer a program path in the shell). `(compile expr)`\n   637\trenders AST, source datoms, expanded datoms, and events. `(reset)` and\n   638\t`(vm ...)` rebuild the whole session, which clears the expander's store with\n   639\tthe evaluator's. `repl-state` lists the macro names the expander's store\n   640\tholds, so an \"unable to resolve `defn`\" is diagnosable at a glance.\n   641\t\n   642\tThe shell's frontends are untouched: `yang.clojure/compile` and\n   643\t`compile-program` lose their `macro-env` arities; `yang.python` and\n   644\t`yang.php` never had any and gain none.\n   645\t\n   646\t### 6.2 `yang.clojure`\n   647\t\n   648\t`defmacro` stays a special form lowering to `(yin/def name <lambda macro?\n   649\ttrue>)`. Deleted: `initial-macro-env`, `shadow-macro-env`,\n   650\t`compile-macro-operands`, `compile-defn`'s native fallback (the `defn` macro\n   651\tis always in the expander's store), the `macro-env` parameter on every\n   652\t`compile-*`, and `:yin/macro-expand` emission. `compile-program` returns\n   653\tAST only.\n   654\t\n   655\t### 6.3 Non-homoiconic frontends\n   656\t\n   657\t`yang.python` and `yang.php` emit `:application` for every call, as they do\n   658\ttoday. A call to a macro defined in Clojure is expanded because the\n   659\texpander's store has it — cross-language macro use costs the frontend\n   660\tnothing. Authoring macros from Python or PHP is the prelude behind a\n   661\t`defmacro` surface and is reserved; the Python tokenizer has no list-literal\n   662\tproduction, so a Python `defn` call is not yet expressible.\n   663\t\n   664\t### 6.4 Build pipelines\n   665\t\n   666\tA build composes the same forwarder between file-backed media\n   667\t(`dao.stream.file.md`), committing `program-in` to `dao.space` first when it\n   668\twants durable provenance (§4.2). The expander's store can be seeded from a\n   669\tcommitted stream with `definitions`; a DaoDB query for\n   670\t`[?e :yin/macro? true]` joined to its `yin/def` is a future producer of the\n   671\tsame map.\n   672\t\n   673\t### 6.5 Cross-platform\n   674\t\n   675\tOne `.cljc`; the expander is maps, vectors, and `reduce`. Symbols from\n   676\t`yin/gensym-sym` are built with `symbol`. The `#?(:cljd …)`-first\n   677\tconditional and `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` catch form\n   678\tare used as elsewhere in v2. Test discovery: confirm \"Testing\n   679\tyin.vm.macro-test\" in the shadow `:node-test` output.\n   680\t\n   681\t---\n   682\t\n   683\t## 7. Phased implementation roadmap\n   684\t\n   685\t### Phase 0 — Spec and codec\n   686\t\n   687\t- This document. `macro-design.md` and `cross-language-macro.md` amended to\n   688\t  point here. The divergence register's macro item becomes: \"user-defined\n   689\t  macros are expanded by a process between media, never by an evaluator;\n   690\t  call sites are ordinary applications; `eval` does not expand.\" Its Macros\n   691\t  section records the deviations of Appendix A.\n   692\t- `yin.vm`: `schema` gains `:yin/root` and `:yin/macro-name`, drops\n   693\t  `:yin/phase-policy`, `:yin/phase`, `:yin/capability`, `:yin/source-call`,\n   694\t  `:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root`, `:yin/error` (event attributes move\n   695\t  to the expander's emitter). `ast->datoms-with-root` emits the root fact;\n   696\t  `index-datoms` honours it (last wins, dangling error); the codec drops the\n   697\t  `:yin/macro-expand` arms and the `:phase-policy` handling.\n   698\t- `ast-walker`: the `:lambda` arm and `datoms->ast` continue to ignore\n   699\t  `:macro?`; nothing else changes. The namespace docstring's \"no macro\n   700\t  branch\" remark becomes the statement of decision 1.\n   701\t- `stream-observer/run-on-stream`: return progress with errors (§5\n   702\t  prerequisite). Test: A forwarded, B throws, retry from the returned\n   703\t  session leaves exactly one A on the destination; same for a throwing\n   704\t  `run-vm` and for a terminal read after a successful batch.\n   705\t- New `test/yin/vm_test.cljc` for the codec: root fact wins; dangling\n   706\t  root errors; heuristic fallback unchanged; `compile` output unchanged\n   707\t  *modulo the root fact* for macro-free programs (the fact is unconditional).\n   708\t\n   709\t### Phase 1 — Expander and yang\n   710\t\n   711\tDeliverables: `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc` (`expand-batch`, `expand`,\n   712\t`definitions`, `invoke`, `bind-params`, `valid-ast?`, `mark-tail`,\n   713\t`run-bounded`, prelude, `stdlib-forms`, `step`); `yang.clojure` per §6.2.\n   714\t\n   715\tTests (`macro_test.cljc`): `defn` via `stdlib-forms` equals the previously\n   716\tnative lowering; definition consumed and replaced by a name literal; a\n   717\tdefinition in an unexecuted branch is harvested; last definition wins;\n   718\t**redefinition as a plain function removes the macro and the next call is\n   719\tforwarded as an application**; outermost-first (`->`-style macro);\n   720\tshadowing by `let`, `fn`, and by a `defn`'s own params without any frontend\n   721\thint; `(fn [m] (m 1))` with `m` a macro is left intact; a macro name in\n   722\toperand position is left as a variable; **an operator macro returning a\n   723\tmacro name, and one returning an inline macro lambda, are both expanded**;\n   724\ta macro emitting a definition fails `:generated-definition`; depth guard\n   725\twith chain; per-expansion bound from node count before emission; per-batch\n   726\tbound; step budget on an infinite body; parked body rejected; **cyclic\n   727\toperand, dangling ref, and a host value in a literal all fail at admission\n   728\tbefore any decode**; invalid output including a host function inside a\n   729\tliteral and an `:eid` key; arity and `&`; **gensyms differ across two\n   730\tbatches with overlapping tempids when ctx is threaded, and are equal on\n   731\treplay of the same ctx**; a stream effect in a body fails with the\n   732\tconstructor error; `mark-tail` on `if`/`let`/lambda spines **and clears a\n   733\tstale flag on marked syntax moved into an operand and into an `if` test**;\n   734\ta macro-free deeply nested batch passes; determinism (two runs, equal\n   735\tvectors); macro-free batch returned equal modulo the root fact; a\n   736\tcorpus-scale batch at 80% of a configured per-batch bound passes; the\n   737\tforwarder driven through `observer/run-on-stream`: `out=ok, log=full`\n   738\tretries only the log; `out=full` retries only the program; repeated `full`;\n   739\tno log medium; **an expansion failure advances the cursor, stages only the\n   740\tevent, and appears in `:errors`**; a forwarder-defect throw does not advance\n   741\tthe cursor.\n   742\t\n   743\t### Phase 2 — REPL composition\n   744\t\n   745\tDeliverables per §6.1. Tests: define in one input, use in the next; use\n   746\tfrom `:python` after defining in `:clojure`; `(reset)` forgets; `(compile)`\n   747\trenders four stages; a runtime error from a macro-named variable in operand\n   748\tposition is an unbound-variable error, not a call; a bad macro input prints\n   749\tits error and the *next* input evaluates normally (no head-of-line block);\n   750\t`repl-state` lists the store's macro names.\n   751\t\n   752\tDeferred to the semantic VM's own Phases 1–2 (`yin.vm.semantic` and\n   753\t`linearize` do not exist yet): the `:semantic` VM type runs the same session\n   754\twith equal values, and a tail-position macro in a 10⁵-iteration loop keeps\n   755\tcontinuation depth 0 — measured by inspecting `k`, since equal values alone\n   756\tdo not prove it.\n   757\t\n   758\t### Phase 3 — Cross-language verification\n   759\t\n   760\tTests (`python_macro_test.clj`, `php_macro_test.clj`): a scalar-signature\n   761\tClojure macro `(defmacro twice [f] (yin/application f [(yin/application f\n   762\t[(yin/literal 1)])]))` called as `twice(inc_fn)` from Python and\n   763\t`twice($inc_fn)` from PHP; `yin/name-of` with a Python string, a Clojure\n   764\tsymbol, and a `:variable`; a PHP `for`-loop variable shadowing a macro name;\n   765\tthe source batch unchanged after expansion; equal values on walker and\n   766\tsemantic. Python `defn` stays reserved until the parser has list literals.\n   767\t\n   768\t### Reserved\n   769\t\n   770\tThe expander as a `yin.vm` program (requires a bounded-child-evaluation\n   771\tprimitive — the general form of `run-bounded`); macro-defining macros\n   772\t(generated definitions, with a precedence rule for definitions introduced\n   773\tmid-batch); macro helpers via expansion of macro bodies before storing;\n   774\tpayload-size bounds at admission for untrusted authors; Shibi at the medium\n   775\tboundary; automatic hygiene; DaoDB-backed store seeding; durable\n   776\tcross-medium provenance and program↔log correspondence as a transactor\n   777\tconcern; expansion memo keyed by call structure (sound because expansion is\n   778\tpure, but each expansion mints its own event and gensyms); Python/PHP\n   779\t`defmacro` surfaces.\n   780\t\n   781\t---\n   782\t\n   783\t## Appendix A. Deviations from prior documents, with reasons\n   784\t\n   785\tFrom `macro-design.md` (v1):\n   786\t\n   787\t1. **AST maps, not eids, as macro arguments.** Every frontend speaks maps;\n   788\t   the datom form is the transport, not the API.\n   789\t2. **Outermost-first, not post-order.** `->`-style macros are impossible\n   790\t   innermost-first, and outermost-first is what makes lexical shadowing\n   791\t   computable without frontend hints.\n   792\t3. **Fixpoint is recursive re-expansion of output**, not \"a pass emits zero\n   793\t   new macro datoms\" — per node rather than per sweep, which is what makes\n   794\t   depth a meaningful guard.\n   795\t4. **No phase policy, no `maybe-recompile-at-boundary`.** Both existed to\n   796\t   describe expansion happening inside an executing VM.\n   797\t\n   798\tFrom `cross-language-macro.md`:\n   799\t\n   800\t5. **Frontends do not discover or coerce.** §3 of that document made the\n   801\t   frontend responsible for knowing which names are macros and for\n   802\t   normalizing arguments; both are the expander's, and a call site is an\n   803\t   ordinary application. Its ordering rule (§4: commit definitions first for\n   804\t   durable ids) is retained.\n   805\t\n   806\tFrom the previous revision of this document — the evaluator-integrated\n   807\tdesign, withdrawn 2026-09-13:\n   808\t\n   809\t6. **No runtime expansion inside evaluators.** That design gave the walker\n   810\t   a `:yin/macro-expand` transition, the semantic VM a `:macro-call`\n   811\t   instruction with ephemeral segments and a retention problem, and the\n   812\t   stack VM `OP_MACRO_EXPAND`. All of it was the cost of running a\n   813\t   syntax-side process inside a semantics-side VM. With expansion on its\n   814\t   own medium the machinery has nowhere to live. What runtime expansion\n   815\t   bought — \"the definition arrived after the program loaded\" — is, in a\n   816\t   stream, \"another batch arrived\".\n   817\t7. **No ledger on the VM, no `:macro-eval` / `:macro-authorize` /\n   818\t   `:capability` options, no `:macro-alloc`, no snapshot fields.** The\n   819\t   expander owns its state and its log writer.\n   820\t8. **No macro flags on closures and no \"macro applied as function\" checks**\n   821\t   at three hot-loop sites (its decision 10). Definitions never reach an\n   822\t   evaluator (decision 5), so there is no closure to flag; a missed macro is\n   823\t   an unbound variable.\n   824\t9. **No `:yin/macro-expand` node type** and no frontend `macro-env`\n   825\t   threading through `yang.clojure`, `yang.python`, `yang.php`, or the\n   826\t   shell. In datoms, operands are already syntax.\n   827\t10. **No shadow hints** (`:yang/shadow-params-operand`,\n   828\t    `:yang/shadow-body-start`, and their proposed promotion to facts).\n   829\t    Outermost-first plus lexical shadow tracking in the expander subsumes\n   830\t    them (§3.2).\n   831\t11. **Self-contained output instead of shared subtrees by reference.** That\n   832\t    design's `:existing` emitter option, structural identity rule,\n   833\t    `:keep-eids?` predicate, and forged-eid check served cross-batch\n   834\t    sharing that is only meaningful for durable ids. Copying is the honest\n   835\t    baseline; sharing returns, if at all, as a transactor concern.\n   836\t12. **Events on a log medium, not interleaved with program.** That design\n   837\t    needed `index-datoms` to exclude event entities from root discovery;\n   838\t    here the evaluator never sees one.\n   839\t13. **Kept from that revision:** the explicit `:yin/root` fact (now a codec\n   840\t    improvement on its own merits); the closed-vocabulary validator with\n   841\t    recursive plain-data checks; dual guards and the fixed traversal order;\n   842\t    `mark-tail` as an expander pass; the fuelled body runner; closed syntax\n   843\t    transformers with no store access; `:yin/macro-name` and logical\n   844\t    `:yin/timestamp`; the loader-adapter (never `comp`) correction to the\n   845\t    semantic spec, which stands independently.\n   846\t\n   847\t## Appendix B. Risks the implementer should watch\n   848\t\n   849\t- Land `:yin/root` in the codec before the expander; until then an expanded\n   850\t  batch's root is found by heuristic and copy order matters.\n   851\t- Definitions must be *replaced*, not merely recorded; a forwarded\n   852\t  `(yin/def defn <lambda>)` would give the evaluator a plain function named\n   853\t  `defn` — the exact footgun decision 5 exists to prevent. The Phase 1 final\n   854\t  scan is the guard.\n   855\t- The shadow set must include the params of a lambda in *operator* position\n   856\t  before descending into its body; `let` and `do` lower to exactly that\n   857\t  shape.\n   858\t- `mark-tail` must recurse through `:if` branches and immediately-applied\n   859\t  lambdas; the semantic-VM tail-loop test is the guard.\n   860\t- `run-bounded` must check `:parked` and `:ready-queue` explicitly:\n   861\t  `:vm/park` sets `:halted? true` and `halted?` does not inspect them.\n   862\t- Do not let `:staged` be dropped on `full`; `run-on-stream` relies on\n   863\t  `ready?` staying false until the append succeeds.\n   864\t- yang deletions in §6.2 remove the native `defn` lowering; every test that\n   865\t  used `defn` without `stdlib-forms` now depends on the expander's seeded\n   866\t  store.\n   867\t- Admission must run cycle and dangling-ref checks over the *index*, never\n   868\t  by calling `datoms->ast` first: the decoder is recursive and overflows on\n   869\t  a cycle before any check runs.\n   870\t- `mark-tail` is a recompute over the final tree, not an additive pass over\n   871\t  each expansion's output; an additive pass leaves stale flags on moved\n   872\t  syntax.\n   873\t- Land the `run-on-stream` progress-on-error change before the REPL wires\n   874\t  the expander, or a forwarder-defect throw after a successful batch\n   875\t  duplicates that batch on retry.\n   876\t\n   877\t## Appendix C. Round-2 review findings and their resolution\n   878\t\n   879\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   880\t|---|---|---|\n   881\t| 1 | Dual-medium flush duplicates the program on `out=ok, log=full` (astra P1-2, glm P2-2) | §5 per-medium staging; at-most-once per medium stated; atomicity explicitly not promised |\n   882\t| 2 | Failures never reach the log; deterministic failure head-of-line blocks `program-in` (glm P2-1, astra P2-4) | Decision 11; §3.1 `:error` result carrying the event and `:ctx`; §5 stages only the log, advances, reports `:errors`; §6.1 REPL prints and continues; throw reserved for forwarder defects |\n   883\t| 3 | Later exception replays earlier forwarded batches (astra P1-1) | Accepted as a defect of `run-on-stream`, not of this design; §5 prerequisite and Phase 0 deliverable on `stream-observer`: return progress with the error |\n   884\t| 4 | Operator rewritten into a macro name is not re-expanded (astra P1-3) | §3.2 re-checks the rebuilt node once at the same depth |\n   885\t| 5 | Generated definitions neither installed nor replaced (astra P1-3) | §3.2 rejected in v1 as `:generated-definition`; macro-defining macros reserved |\n   886\t| 6 | Stale store after plain redefinition expands dead code (glm P2-3) | §3.1 harvest is authoritative for every `yin/def`: plain lambda `dissoc`es; §2.2 two-namespaces paragraph; Phase 1 test |\n   887\t| 7 | Alloc not monotonic; gensyms repeat across batches (astra P2-5, glm P3-4) | §3.1 seed includes `(:next-eid alloc)`; retry reuses staged allocation |\n   888\t| 8 | Final scan must be scope-aware (astra P2-6, glm P3-8) | §3.1 step 7 scans only for `:macro?` lambdas; application fixpoint is by construction via #4 |\n   889\t| 9 | Tail marking must clear stale flags; lambda rule ambiguous (astra P2-7) | §3.4 whole-tree recompute with per-node context rules; lambda body always tail; `linearize` owns inlining |\n   890\t| 10 | Provenance identity: same tempid in two batches, generated syntax, program↔log correspondence, event schema home, anonymous macros (astra P2-8) | §4.1 `:yin/source-batch`; nested `source-call` into the log copy; `expansion-root` names the log copy, correspondence not promised; `macro/event-schema`; `:yin/macro-name` absent for inline lambdas |\n   891\t| 11 | Output validation alone does not close the sandbox input (astra P2-9) | §3.1 step 1 admission validates ground values; §3.3 validates operands going in; `:eval`/prelude trust contract stated; fuel and payload limits recorded as known v1 limits |\n   892\t| 12 | Cycle detection after recursive decode overflows; `eid(node)` undefined on maps (astra P2-10, glm P3-8) | §3.1 admission over the index; working-representation paragraph; Appendix B |\n   893\t| 13 | \"As in Clojure\" is wrong for syntactic harvest; \"helpers are other macros\" unsupported; macro/runtime namespace relation unstated (astra P3-11) | §3.1 policy stated on its own terms; §3.3 helpers are prelude only, body expansion reserved; §2.2 two namespaces |\n   894\t| 14 | `program-out` `m = ev` would be a cross-medium ref; event attrs dropped from schema but still emitted (glm P3-5) | Decision 9 and §4.1: `program-out` carries `default-op`; `macro/event-schema` |\n   895\t| 15 | Root fact unconditional vs \"compile output unchanged\" (glm P3-6) | Phase 0 test reworded \"modulo the root fact\" |\n   896\t| 16 | Phase 2 `:semantic` test has an unstated prerequisite (glm P3-7) | Phase 2 defers it to the semantic spec's Phases 1–2, with the continuation-depth measurement |\n   897\t| 17 | Missed-macro diagnostics (glm P3-9) | §6.1 `repl-state` lists the store's names |\n   898\t| — | Qualify \"never holds VM control state\" (astra, layering) | §1.2 table: the *program* evaluator's state |\n   899\t| — | Later batch ≠ expansion inside a loaded continuation (astra P3-11) | Appendix A #6 already states the limit; decision 11 makes the consumed-batch semantics explicit |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
